/* AI brain Q&A pack 06: language & writing */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what is a noun | define noun | noun meaning | what are nouns", "A **noun** is a word for a person, place, thing or idea — like *teacher*, *Paris*, *apple* or *happiness*."],
  ["what is a proper noun | proper noun vs common noun | difference between proper and common nouns", "A **proper noun** names one specific person, place or thing and starts with a capital letter (*Maria*, *Tokyo*, *Monday*). A **common noun** is a general word for any one of a kind (*girl*, *city*, *day*)."],
  ["what is a collective noun | examples of collective nouns | collective noun meaning", "A **collective noun** names a group as one unit — like a *team* of players, a *flock* of birds, a *pack* of wolves or a *swarm* of bees."],
  ["what is an abstract noun | abstract noun examples | abstract vs concrete noun", "An **abstract noun** names something you can't touch or see, like *love*, *courage*, *freedom* or *time*. A concrete noun is something physical, like *chair* or *dog*."],
  ["what is a verb | define verb | verb meaning | what are verbs", "A **verb** is an action or state-of-being word — like *run*, *think*, *eat* or *is*. Every complete sentence needs one: *The cat **sleeps**.*"],
  ["what is a helping verb | auxiliary verb meaning | what are helping verbs", "A **helping (auxiliary) verb** works with a main verb to show tense or mood — like *is*, *have*, *will*, *can* and *should*. Example: *She **has been** reading.*"],
  ["what is an adjective | define adjective | adjective meaning | what do adjectives do", "An **adjective** describes a noun — it tells *what kind*, *which one* or *how many*. Example: *the **tall**, **green** tree* or *three **happy** puppies*."],
  ["what is an adverb | define adverb | adverb meaning | what do adverbs do", "An **adverb** describes a verb, adjective or another adverb — often telling *how*, *when*, *where* or *how much*. Many end in *-ly*: *She sang **beautifully**.*"],
  ["what is a pronoun | define pronoun | pronoun meaning | examples of pronouns", "A **pronoun** takes the place of a noun so you don't repeat it — like *he*, *she*, *it*, *they*, *we* and *you*. Example: *Sam lost **his** hat, so **he** looked for **it**.*"],
  ["what is a preposition | define preposition | preposition meaning | examples of prepositions", "A **preposition** shows how a noun relates to something else, often in place or time — like *in*, *on*, *under*, *before*, *with* and *between*. Example: *The keys are **under** the mat.*"],
  ["what is a conjunction | define conjunction | conjunction meaning | what are fanboys", "A **conjunction** joins words or ideas. The main ones spell **FANBOYS**: *for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so*. Example: *I was tired, **but** I finished my homework.*"],
  ["what is an interjection | define interjection | interjection examples", "An **interjection** is a word that shows sudden feeling, often with an exclamation mark — like *Wow!*, *Oops!*, *Ouch!* or *Hooray!*"],
  ["what is an article in grammar | what are articles | definite and indefinite articles", "**Articles** are little words before nouns: *the* is the **definite** article (a specific thing) and *a*/*an* are **indefinite** articles (any one). Example: *I saw **a** dog. **The** dog was huge.*"],
  ["what are the parts of speech | list the parts of speech | 8 parts of speech | how many parts of speech are there", "The **8 parts of speech** are: noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction and interjection. (Some lists add articles/determiners as a ninth.)"],
  ["their there or they're | difference between their there and they're | when to use their | when to use there", "**Their** shows ownership (*their dog*), **there** is a place or pointer (*over there*, *there is*), and **they're** means *they are* (*they're late*). Tip: if you can swap in *they are*, use **they're**."],
  ["your or you're | difference between your and you're | when to use you're", "**Your** shows ownership (*your book*). **You're** is short for *you are* (*you're awesome*). Test it: if *you are* fits, write **you're**."],
  ["its or it's | difference between its and it's | when to use it's | its vs it's", "**It's** means *it is* or *it has* (*it's raining*). **Its** shows ownership with no apostrophe (*the dog wagged its tail*). If *it is* fits, use **it's**."],
  ["affect or effect | difference between affect and effect | affect vs effect", "Usually, **affect** is a verb (to influence) and **effect** is a noun (the result). Example: *The rain **affected** the game; the **effect** was a muddy field.* Memory trick: **A**ffect = **A**ction, **E**ffect = **E**nd result."],
  ["then or than | difference between then and than | then vs than", "**Than** is for comparing (*taller **than** me*). **Then** is about time or order (*we ate, **then** we left*). Trick: th**a**n goes with comp**a**re, th**e**n goes with wh**e**n."],
  ["to too or two | difference between to too and two | when to use too", "**To** shows direction or goes before a verb (*go **to** school*, *to swim*). **Too** means *also* or *excessively* (*me **too**, **too** hot*). **Two** is the number 2."],
  ["a or an | when to use a or an | a vs an rule", "Use **an** before a vowel *sound* and **a** before a consonant *sound*. It's about sound, not spelling: ***an** hour*, ***an** apple*, but ***a** unicorn*, ***a** house*."],
  ["who or whom | difference between who and whom | when to use whom", "Use **who** for the subject (the one doing it) and **whom** for the object (the one receiving it). Trick: if you'd answer with *he*, use **who**; if *him*, use **whom** — *Whom did you call? I called him.*"],
  ["fewer or less | difference between fewer and less | fewer vs less", "Use **fewer** for things you can count (*fewer cookies*, *fewer people*) and **less** for things you can't count (*less water*, *less time*)."],
  ["lose or loose | difference between lose and loose | lose vs loose", "**Lose** (one *o*) means to misplace or not win (*don't lose your keys*). **Loose** (two *o*s) means not tight (*a loose shoelace*)."],
  ["accept or except | difference between accept and except | accept vs except", "**Accept** means to receive or agree (*I accept your offer*). **Except** means *but not* (*everyone came except Tom*)."],
  ["good or well | difference between good and well | when to use well", "**Good** is an adjective that describes nouns (*a good book*). **Well** is usually an adverb that describes verbs (*she plays well*). Say *I did **well** on the test*, not *I did good*."],
  ["me or i | when to use me or i | john and me or john and i", "Remove the other person and see what sounds right. *(Sam and) **I** went to the park* — because *I went*. *Mom drove Sam and **me*** — because *Mom drove me*."],
  ["lay or lie | difference between lay and lie | lay vs lie", "**Lie** means to recline (no object): *I lie down.* **Lay** means to put something down (needs an object): *Lay the book on the table.* Tricky bit: the past tense of *lie* is *lay*."],
  ["whose or who's | difference between whose and who's", "**Who's** means *who is* or *who has* (*who's coming?*). **Whose** shows ownership (*whose jacket is this?*)."],
  ["were or where | difference between were and where | we're were where", "**Were** is a past-tense verb (*we were happy*), **where** asks about a place (*where is it?*), and **we're** means *we are* (*we're ready*)."],
  ["advice or advise | difference between advice and advise", "**Advice** is a noun — the suggestion itself (*good advice*). **Advise** is the verb — giving it (*I advise you to rest*)."],
  ["compliment or complement | difference between compliment and complement", "A **compliment** is a kind remark (*nice shoes!*). A **complement** is something that completes or goes well with something (*the sauce complements the pasta*)."],
  ["principal or principle | difference between principal and principle", "A **principal** is the head of a school, or the main thing (*the principal reason*). A **principle** is a rule or belief (*honesty is my principle*). Trick: the princi**pal** is your **pal**."],
  ["stationary or stationery | difference between stationary and stationery", "**Stationary** (with an **a**) means not moving — *a parked car*. **Stationery** (with an **e**) is writing paper and envelopes — think **e** for **e**nvelope."],
  ["could of or could have | is it could of or could've | should of or should have", "It's always **could have**, **should have** and **would have** (or *could've*). *Could of* is a mistake that comes from how *could've* sounds when spoken."],
  ["what is a comma used for | when to use a comma | comma rules | how to use commas", "Use a **comma** to separate items in a list (*apples, pears, and plums*), before a joining word linking two sentences (*I ran, but I missed the bus*), after an intro phrase (*After lunch, we played*), and around extra info (*My dog, Max, is silly*)."],
  ["what is the oxford comma | serial comma meaning | should i use the oxford comma", "The **Oxford comma** is the comma before *and* or *or* in a list of three or more: *red, white**,** and blue*. It's optional in many styles, but it can prevent confusion — just be consistent."],
  ["when to use a semicolon | what is a semicolon | how to use a semicolon | semicolon rules", "A **semicolon (;)** joins two complete sentences that are closely related, without *and* or *but*: *It was late; we went home.* It can also separate list items that already contain commas."],
  ["when to use a colon | what is a colon in grammar | how to use a colon | colon rules", "A **colon (:)** introduces something that follows — a list, an explanation or a quote. Example: *Bring three things: a pen, a notebook and a snack.* The part before the colon should be a complete sentence."],
  ["when to use an apostrophe | how to use apostrophes | apostrophe rules | what is an apostrophe", "An **apostrophe (')** does two jobs: shows possession (*Sara's bike*, *the dogs' bowls*) and marks missing letters in contractions (*don't* = do not). Don't use it to make plurals — it's *apples*, not *apple's*."],
  ["apostrophe with plural possessive | where does the apostrophe go for plurals | students or students'", "For a plural ending in *s*, put the apostrophe after the *s*: *the students' lockers*. For a plural that doesn't end in *s*, add *'s*: *the children's toys*."],
  ["when to use a hyphen | what is a hyphen | hyphen vs dash", "A **hyphen (-)** joins words into one idea, like *well-known* or *twenty-one*. A **dash (—)** is longer and sets off a sudden break or extra thought — *like this*."],
  ["when to use quotation marks | how to use quotation marks | where does punctuation go with quotes", "Use **quotation marks** around someone's exact words or titles of short works. In American English, commas and periods go inside: *She said, \"Let's go.\"*"],
  ["when to use an exclamation mark | what is an exclamation point", "An **exclamation mark (!)** ends a sentence that shows strong feeling or a command — *Watch out!* or *We won!* Use them sparingly so they keep their punch."],
  ["what is a question mark | when to use a question mark", "A **question mark (?)** ends a direct question: *Where are you going?* Don't use one for an indirect question like *I wonder where you're going.*"],
  ["what are parentheses used for | when to use brackets in writing | how to use parentheses", "**Parentheses ( )** hold extra information that's helpful but not essential: *My cousin (the one who lives in Ohio) is visiting.* The sentence should still make sense without it."],
  ["what is an ellipsis | when to use an ellipsis | what does ... mean", "An **ellipsis (…)** is three dots that show something left out of a quote, or a pause or trailing thought: *Well… I'm not sure.*"],
  ["what is a sentence | define sentence | what makes a complete sentence", "A **sentence** is a group of words that expresses a complete thought. It needs a **subject** (who/what) and a **predicate** (verb part), starts with a capital letter and ends with punctuation: *The bird sang.*"],
  ["what are the 4 types of sentences | types of sentences | declarative interrogative imperative exclamatory", "The four types are: **declarative** (statement — *I like tea.*), **interrogative** (question — *Do you like tea?*), **imperative** (command — *Pour the tea.*) and **exclamatory** (strong feeling — *I love tea!*)."],
  ["what is a simple compound and complex sentence | compound sentence meaning | complex sentence meaning", "A **simple** sentence has one main clause (*I ran.*). A **compound** sentence joins two main clauses (*I ran, and she walked.*). A **complex** sentence has a main clause plus a dependent one (*I ran because I was late.*)."],
  ["what is a run on sentence | how to fix a run on sentence | run-on sentence", "A **run-on sentence** jams two complete sentences together without proper punctuation: *I love pizza I eat it daily.* Fix it with a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus *and/but/so*."],
  ["what is a sentence fragment | fragment meaning grammar | how to fix a fragment", "A **fragment** is an incomplete sentence — it's missing a subject, a verb, or a complete thought: *Because it was raining.* Fix it by finishing the thought: *We stayed in because it was raining.*"],
  ["what is a comma splice | comma splice meaning", "A **comma splice** joins two complete sentences with only a comma: *It was cold, I wore a coat.* Fix it with a period, a semicolon, or a joining word: *It was cold, so I wore a coat.*"],
  ["what is a subject and predicate | subject vs predicate | what is the subject of a sentence", "The **subject** is who or what the sentence is about; the **predicate** tells what the subject does or is. In *My little brother laughed loudly*, the subject is *my little brother* and the predicate is *laughed loudly*."],
  ["what is a clause | independent vs dependent clause | dependent clause meaning", "A **clause** is a group of words with a subject and a verb. An **independent clause** can stand alone (*I smiled*); a **dependent clause** can't (*when she arrived*)."],
  ["what is a phrase in grammar | phrase vs clause", "A **phrase** is a group of words working together but without both a subject and verb — like *in the morning* or *the big red balloon*. A clause has a subject and verb."],
  ["what is a paragraph | define paragraph | how long is a paragraph", "A **paragraph** is a group of sentences about one main idea. It usually starts with a **topic sentence**, adds supporting details, and ends with a closing sentence — often 3 to 8 sentences."],
  ["what is a topic sentence | how to write a topic sentence", "A **topic sentence** states the main idea of a paragraph, usually as the first sentence. Example: *Dogs make wonderful pets for three reasons.* Everything else in the paragraph should support it."],
  ["what is subject verb agreement | subject verb agreement rules", "**Subject-verb agreement** means a singular subject takes a singular verb and a plural subject takes a plural verb: *The dog **barks**.* / *The dogs **bark**.*"],
  ["what is past present and future tense | verb tenses explained | what are tenses", "**Tense** shows when something happens. **Past**: *I walked.* **Present**: *I walk.* **Future**: *I will walk.* Each also has continuous and perfect forms, like *I was walking* or *I have walked*."],
  ["what is active and passive voice | active vs passive voice | what is passive voice", "In **active voice**, the subject does the action: *The cat chased the mouse.* In **passive voice**, the subject receives it: *The mouse was chased by the cat.* Active is usually clearer and stronger."],
  ["what is a contraction | contraction examples | contractions in english", "A **contraction** shortens two words into one using an apostrophe: *do not → don't*, *I am → I'm*, *they will → they'll*, *cannot → can't*."],
  ["what is a plural | how to make words plural | plural rules", "A **plural** means more than one. Most words add *-s* (*cats*); words ending in *s, x, ch, sh* add *-es* (*boxes*); consonant + *y* becomes *-ies* (*babies*). Some are irregular: *child → children*."],
  ["what is the plural of mouse | plural of goose | irregular plurals examples", "Some plurals are irregular: *mouse → mice*, *goose → geese*, *child → children*, *tooth → teeth*, *person → people*, and *sheep*, *deer* and *fish* usually stay the same."],
  ["what is a prefix | prefix meaning | prefix examples", "A **prefix** is added to the start of a word to change its meaning: *un-* (not) in *unhappy*, *re-* (again) in *redo*, *pre-* (before) in *preview*."],
  ["what is a suffix | suffix meaning | suffix examples", "A **suffix** is added to the end of a word to change its meaning or type: *-ful* in *hopeful*, *-ness* in *kindness*, *-ly* in *quickly*."],
  ["what is a root word | root word meaning | base word", "A **root word** is the core part of a word that carries its meaning. In *unhelpful*, the root is *help*. Many roots come from Latin or Greek, like *bio* (life) and *graph* (write)."],
  ["what is a compound word | compound word examples", "A **compound word** is two words joined to make a new one: *sun + flower = sunflower*, *rain + bow = rainbow*, *foot + ball = football*."],
  ["what is a comparative and superlative | comparative vs superlative | big bigger biggest", "A **comparative** compares two things (*bigger*, *more careful*); a **superlative** compares three or more (*biggest*, *most careful*). Short words add *-er/-est*; long ones use *more/most*."],
  ["what is a double negative | is a double negative wrong", "A **double negative** uses two negatives in one idea, like *I don't have no money*. In standard English they cancel out, so say *I don't have any money* or *I have no money*."],
  ["when do you capitalize words | capitalization rules | what words should be capitalized", "Capitalize the first word of a sentence, the word *I*, names of people and places, days and months, holidays, and the main words in titles: *On Monday, Ana and I visited Rome.*"],
  ["british vs american spelling | colour or color | british vs american english", "British and American English differ in some spellings: *colour/color*, *centre/center*, *organise/organize*, *travelled/traveled*. Both are correct — just pick one style and stay consistent."],
  ["what is a direct object | direct object meaning | indirect object meaning", "A **direct object** receives the action of the verb: *She kicked **the ball**.* An **indirect object** is who receives the direct object: *She gave **me** the ball.*"],
  ["what is a possessive pronoun | possessive pronouns list", "**Possessive pronouns** show ownership without an apostrophe: *mine, yours, his, hers, its, ours, theirs*. Example: *That bike is **hers**.*"],
  ["what is an infinitive | infinitive meaning | what is a split infinitive", "An **infinitive** is *to* + a verb: *to run*, *to sing*. A **split infinitive** puts a word in between, like *to boldly go* — it's perfectly fine in modern English."],
  ["can you end a sentence with a preposition | is it ok to end a sentence with a preposition", "Yes! Ending a sentence with a preposition is fine in modern English. *Who are you talking to?* sounds much more natural than *To whom are you talking?*"],
  ["can you start a sentence with and or but | is it ok to begin a sentence with because", "Yes, you can start a sentence with *And*, *But* or *Because* — good writers do it all the time. Just make sure the sentence is complete: *But I wasn't finished yet.*"],
  ["what is grammar | why is grammar important | define grammar", "**Grammar** is the set of rules for how words fit together to make sense in a language. Good grammar makes your writing clear, so readers understand exactly what you mean."],
  ["what is punctuation | why is punctuation important | punctuation marks list", "**Punctuation** marks like . , ? ! ; : ' and \" help readers know where to pause and how to read a sentence. Compare *Let's eat, Grandma!* with *Let's eat Grandma!* — commas save lives."],
  ["what are the vowels | what is a vowel | what is a consonant | vowels and consonants", "The **vowels** in English are *A, E, I, O, U* (and sometimes *Y*). All the other letters are **consonants**. Every syllable has a vowel sound."],
  ["what is a syllable | how to count syllables", "A **syllable** is a single beat of sound in a word, each with one vowel sound. *Cat* has 1, *pen-cil* has 2, *el-e-phant* has 3. Clap along as you say the word to count them."],
  ["how many letters are in the alphabet | how many letters in the english alphabet", "The English alphabet has **26 letters**: 5 regular vowels (A, E, I, O, U) and 21 consonants."],
  ["what is the longest word in english | longest english word", "A famous one is *pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis* (45 letters), a lung disease from fine dust. A long everyday word is *antidisestablishmentarianism* (28 letters)."],
  ["what is the most common word in english | most used english word", "The most common word in English is ***the***. Other top words include *be*, *to*, *of*, *and*, *a* and *in*."],
  ["what is a pangram | sentence with every letter of the alphabet | the quick brown fox", "A **pangram** is a sentence that uses every letter of the alphabet. The classic: *The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.*"],
  ["difference between ie and eg | ie vs eg | what does e.g. mean | what does i.e. mean", "**e.g.** means *for example* (*fruits, e.g. apples and pears*). **i.e.** means *that is* or *in other words* (*the biggest planet, i.e. Jupiter*)."],
  ["what does etc mean | how to use etc", "**Etc.** is short for the Latin *et cetera*, meaning *and so on*. Use it at the end of a list: *pens, pencils, erasers, etc.*"],
  ["what is slang | slang meaning | examples of slang", "**Slang** is very informal language used in casual talk, often by a certain group or generation — like *cool*, *chill* or *GOAT*. It's fun in conversation but usually avoided in formal writing."],
  ["what is an idiom | idiom meaning | idiom examples", "An **idiom** is a phrase whose meaning isn't obvious from its words. *It's raining cats and dogs* means it's raining hard; *break a leg* means good luck."],
  ["what does break a leg mean | meaning of break a leg", "*Break a leg* is a friendly way to say **good luck**, especially before a performance. Theater folks traditionally avoid saying *good luck* directly!"],
  ["what does piece of cake mean | meaning of piece of cake idiom", "*A piece of cake* means something is **very easy**. Example: *The quiz was a piece of cake.*"],
  ["what does once in a blue moon mean | meaning of once in a blue moon", "*Once in a blue moon* means **very rarely**. Example: *My brother cleans his room once in a blue moon.*"],
  ["what does spill the beans mean | meaning of spill the beans", "*Spill the beans* means to **reveal a secret**. Example: *Don't spill the beans about the surprise party!*"],
  ["what does under the weather mean | meaning of feeling under the weather", "*Under the weather* means **feeling a little sick**. Example: *I'm staying home today; I feel under the weather.*"],
  ["what does hit the books mean | meaning of hit the books", "*Hit the books* means **to study hard**. Example: *I have a test tomorrow, so I need to hit the books.*"],
  ["what is a simile | define simile | simile examples | simile meaning", "A **simile** compares two different things using *like* or *as*. Examples: *as brave as a lion*, *her smile was like sunshine*."],
  ["what is a metaphor | define metaphor | metaphor examples | metaphor meaning", "A **metaphor** compares two things by saying one *is* the other, without *like* or *as*. Examples: *Time is a thief.* *The classroom was a zoo.*"],
  ["simile vs metaphor | difference between simile and metaphor", "Both compare two unlike things. A **simile** uses *like* or *as* (*he runs like the wind*); a **metaphor** says one thing *is* another (*he is the wind*)."],
  ["what is alliteration | define alliteration | alliteration examples", "**Alliteration** is repeating the same first sound in nearby words. Example: *Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers* or *silly snakes slither slowly*."],
  ["what is onomatopoeia | define onomatopoeia | onomatopoeia examples | words that sound like what they mean", "**Onomatopoeia** is a word that sounds like the noise it describes — like *buzz*, *sizzle*, *boom*, *meow*, *splash* and *crunch*."],
  ["what is personification | define personification | personification examples", "**Personification** gives human qualities to non-human things. Examples: *The wind whispered through the trees.* *The sun smiled down on us.*"],
  ["what is hyperbole | define hyperbole | hyperbole examples | how do you pronounce hyperbole", "**Hyperbole** (say *hy-PER-buh-lee*) is huge exaggeration for effect. Examples: *I've told you a million times!* *This bag weighs a ton.*"],
  ["what is a haiku | define haiku | haiku rules | how many syllables in a haiku", "A **haiku** is a short Japanese-style poem with **3 lines** of **5, 7 and 5 syllables**, often about nature or a single moment.\nExample:\n*Soft snow on the roof (5)*\n*the whole town is holding still (7)*\n*one bird starts to sing (5)*"],
  ["what is a limerick | define limerick | limerick rules | limerick example", "A **limerick** is a funny 5-line poem with an **AABBA** rhyme scheme and a bouncy rhythm.\nExample:\n*A cat with a fondness for cheese*\n*Would sneak to the fridge with great ease.*\n*She'd nibble a slice,*\n*Then blame it on mice,*\n*And purr, \"Just one more, if you please!\"*"],
  ["what is a sonnet | define sonnet | sonnet rules | how many lines in a sonnet", "A **sonnet** is a 14-line poem, usually in a rhythm called *iambic pentameter* (10 syllables per line, da-DUM x5). A Shakespearean sonnet rhymes **ABAB CDCD EFEF GG**, ending with a rhyming couplet."],
  ["what is an acrostic poem | how to write an acrostic poem", "In an **acrostic poem**, the first letters of each line spell a word down the side. For *SUN*: *Shining high above / Up in the morning sky / Never stops glowing.*"],
  ["what is free verse | free verse poem meaning", "**Free verse** is poetry with no fixed rhyme or rhythm. It flows like natural speech and uses line breaks, images and sound to create its effect."],
  ["what is a cinquain | cinquain poem", "A **cinquain** is a 5-line poem. A popular form uses 2, 4, 6, 8 and 2 syllables, or a word pattern: 1 noun, 2 adjectives, 3 action words, a 4-word feeling, then 1 synonym."],
  ["what is a stanza | define stanza | stanza meaning", "A **stanza** is a group of lines in a poem, like a paragraph in prose. A 2-line stanza is a *couplet*, 3 lines a *tercet*, and 4 lines a *quatrain*."],
  ["what is a rhyme scheme | how to find rhyme scheme | what does abab mean", "A **rhyme scheme** is the pattern of rhymes at the ends of lines, labeled with letters. Lines that rhyme get the same letter: *cat (A), tree (B), hat (A), sea (B)* is **ABAB**."],
  ["what is rhythm in poetry | what is meter in poetry | iambic pentameter meaning", "**Meter** is the beat pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a poem. **Iambic pentameter** has five *da-DUM* beats per line: *Shall I | com-PARE | thee TO | a SUM | mer's DAY?*"],
  ["what is imagery | define imagery in writing | imagery examples", "**Imagery** is descriptive language that appeals to the five senses so readers can picture, hear, smell, taste or feel a scene. Example: *The warm bread crackled as I tore it open, filling the kitchen with a buttery smell.*"],
  ["what is assonance | define assonance", "**Assonance** is repeating vowel sounds in nearby words. Example: *The rain in Spain stays mainly in the plain.*"],
  ["what is consonance | define consonance", "**Consonance** is repeating consonant sounds, often in the middle or end of words. Example: *Mike likes his bike.*"],
  ["what is an oxymoron | oxymoron examples | define oxymoron", "An **oxymoron** puts two opposite words side by side. Examples: *jumbo shrimp*, *deafening silence*, *bittersweet*, *awfully good*."],
  ["what is irony | define irony | irony examples", "**Irony** is when the opposite of what you expect happens, or when words mean the opposite of what's said. Example: a fire station burning down, or saying *What lovely weather!* during a storm."],
  ["what is foreshadowing | define foreshadowing", "**Foreshadowing** is a hint about what will happen later in a story. Example: dark clouds gathering before something bad happens to the hero."],
  ["what is a flashback in a story | flashback meaning", "A **flashback** is a scene that jumps back in time to show something that happened earlier, helping explain a character or event."],
  ["what is a pun | pun examples | define pun", "A **pun** is a joke that plays on a word with two meanings or similar sounds. Example: *I used to be a baker, but I couldn't make enough dough.*"],
  ["what is a synonym | define synonym | synonym examples", "A **synonym** is a word that means the same or nearly the same as another word. Examples: *happy / joyful*, *big / large*, *fast / quick*."],
  ["what is an antonym | define antonym | antonym examples | opposite words", "An **antonym** is a word with the opposite meaning. Examples: *hot / cold*, *up / down*, *brave / cowardly*."],
  ["what is a homophone | define homophone | homophone examples", "**Homophones** are words that sound the same but have different meanings and often different spellings: *flower / flour*, *knight / night*, *sea / see*, *their / there / they're*."],
  ["what is a homonym | homonym vs homophone | what is a homograph", "**Homonyms** are words that share the same spelling or sound but differ in meaning — like *bat* (animal) and *bat* (for baseball). **Homographs** are spelled the same but may sound different: *lead* (a metal) and *lead* (to guide)."],
  ["what is an acronym | define acronym | acronym examples", "An **acronym** is a word made from the first letters of other words and said as a word: *NASA* (National Aeronautics and Space Administration), *SCUBA*, *laser*. If you say the letters separately, like *FBI*, it's an **initialism**."],
  ["what is an abbreviation | abbreviation examples", "An **abbreviation** is a shortened form of a word or phrase: *Dr.* for Doctor, *Mon.* for Monday, *etc.* for et cetera, *km* for kilometer."],
  ["what is a palindrome | define palindrome | palindrome examples | words that read the same backwards", "A **palindrome** reads the same forwards and backwards. Words: *racecar*, *level*, *kayak*, *noon*. Sentence: *A man, a plan, a canal: Panama!*"],
  ["what is an anagram | anagram examples | define anagram", "An **anagram** rearranges the letters of a word to make a new one. Examples: *listen → silent*, *heart → earth*, *dormitory → dirty room*."],
  ["what is a portmanteau | portmanteau words examples", "A **portmanteau** blends two words into one: *breakfast + lunch = brunch*, *smoke + fog = smog*, *motor + hotel = motel*."],
  ["what is a tongue twister | give me a tongue twister | tell me a tongue twister", "Try this tongue twister: *She sells seashells by the seashore.* Or a harder one: *Red lorry, yellow lorry* — say it five times fast!"],
  ["what is an oxford dictionary | what is a dictionary | what is a thesaurus | dictionary vs thesaurus", "A **dictionary** tells you what words mean, how to spell and pronounce them. A **thesaurus** gives synonyms and antonyms, helping you find a better word."],
  ["what is a plot | define plot | parts of a plot | plot diagram", "The **plot** is the sequence of events in a story. It usually has five parts: **exposition** (setup), **rising action**, **climax** (turning point), **falling action**, and **resolution** (ending)."],
  ["what is a climax in a story | climax meaning in literature", "The **climax** is the most exciting, intense moment of a story — the turning point where the main conflict comes to a head."],
  ["what is the setting of a story | define setting", "The **setting** is where and when a story takes place — like *a spooky castle on a stormy night* or *a busy city in the year 3000*."],
  ["what is a protagonist | what is an antagonist | protagonist vs antagonist", "The **protagonist** is the main character, usually the hero. The **antagonist** is the character or force working against them — like the villain."],
  ["what is the theme of a story | define theme | how to find the theme", "The **theme** is the big message or lesson of a story, like *friendship matters* or *be true to yourself*. Ask: *What did the main character learn?*"],
  ["what is conflict in a story | types of conflict in literature", "**Conflict** is the problem or struggle in a story. Common types: character vs. character, character vs. self, character vs. nature, and character vs. society."],
  ["what is point of view | first person vs third person | what is first person point of view", "**Point of view** is who tells the story. **First person** uses *I* and *we* (the narrator is in the story). **Second person** uses *you*. **Third person** uses *he*, *she*, *they* (an outside narrator)."],
  ["what is a narrator | define narrator", "The **narrator** is the voice telling the story. They might be a character in it (first person) or an outside voice (third person)."],
  ["what is fiction | what is nonfiction | fiction vs nonfiction", "**Fiction** is made-up stories from the imagination (novels, fairy tales). **Nonfiction** is factual writing about real people, events and information (biographies, news, textbooks)."],
  ["what is a genre | types of genres | literary genres", "A **genre** is a category of writing with shared features. Examples: mystery, fantasy, science fiction, adventure, romance, horror, historical fiction, biography and poetry."],
  ["what is a fable | fable meaning | what is a myth | what is a legend", "A **fable** is a short story, often with talking animals, that teaches a moral (like *The Tortoise and the Hare*). A **myth** explains nature or beliefs with gods and heroes. A **legend** is a story about a hero that may be based loosely on real history."],
  ["what is a moral of a story | moral meaning", "The **moral** is the lesson a story teaches. In *The Tortoise and the Hare*, the moral is *slow and steady wins the race*."],
  ["what is a biography | biography vs autobiography | what is an autobiography | what is a memoir", "A **biography** is a true life story written by someone else. An **autobiography** is one written by the person themselves. A **memoir** is a personal account focused on certain memories or a period of life."],
  ["what is prose | prose vs poetry", "**Prose** is ordinary writing in sentences and paragraphs, like stories and articles. **Poetry** uses lines, rhythm and often rhyme to create sound and feeling."],
  ["what is dialogue | how to write dialogue | how to punctuate dialogue", "**Dialogue** is characters speaking. Put spoken words in quotation marks, start a new paragraph for each new speaker, and put punctuation inside the quotes: *\"Are you coming?\" asked Leo.*"],
  ["what is a cliche | cliche examples | define cliche", "A **cliché** is a phrase used so often it's lost its punch — like *at the end of the day* or *as old as time*. Fresh, specific details make writing stronger."],
  ["what is tone in writing | tone vs mood | what is mood in literature", "**Tone** is the writer's attitude (serious, funny, sarcastic). **Mood** is the feeling the reader gets (spooky, cheerful, tense). A ghost story might have a creepy mood and a playful tone."],
  ["what is a symbol in literature | symbolism meaning", "**Symbolism** is when an object, color or character stands for a bigger idea. A dove often symbolizes peace; a storm can symbolize trouble or anger."],
  ["what is an allusion | define allusion", "An **allusion** is a brief reference to a famous person, story or event. Calling someone a *Romeo* alludes to Shakespeare's romantic hero."],
  ["what is an analogy | analogy examples | define analogy", "An **analogy** explains something by comparing it to something familiar. Example: *A cell is like a tiny factory, with different parts doing different jobs.*"],
  ["what is a euphemism | euphemism examples", "A **euphemism** is a gentle word used instead of a harsh one. Examples: *passed away* instead of *died*, *restroom* instead of *toilet*."],
  ["what is a rhetorical question | rhetorical question meaning", "A **rhetorical question** is asked for effect, not for an answer. Example: *Who doesn't love a sunny day?*"],
  ["what does ubiquitous mean | define ubiquitous | meaning of ubiquitous | ubiquitous definition", "**Ubiquitous** means *found everywhere*. Example: *Smartphones have become ubiquitous — almost everyone has one.*"],
  ["what does empathy mean | define empathy | meaning of empathy | what is empathy", "**Empathy** is the ability to understand and share someone else's feelings — imagining yourself in their shoes. Example: *She showed empathy by listening when her friend was sad.*"],
  ["what does sympathy mean | empathy vs sympathy | define sympathy", "**Sympathy** is feeling sorry for someone's trouble; **empathy** is actually feeling *with* them. Sympathy says *I'm sorry that happened*; empathy says *I understand how that feels*."],
  ["what does procrastinate mean | define procrastinate | meaning of procrastination | what is procrastination", "To **procrastinate** is to keep putting off something you should do. Example: *I procrastinated on my project and had to finish it the night before.*"],
  ["what does resilient mean | define resilient | meaning of resilient | what is resilience", "**Resilient** means able to bounce back after something hard. Example: *Even after losing the game, the team stayed resilient and won the next one.*"],
  ["what does fomo mean | fomo meaning | define fomo", "**FOMO** stands for *Fear Of Missing Out* — the worried feeling that others are having fun without you. Example: *I had FOMO when I saw photos of the party.*"],
  ["what does lol mean | lol meaning", "**LOL** means *laugh out loud* — used in texts when something is funny."],
  ["what does brb mean | brb meaning", "**BRB** means *be right back* — you're stepping away for a moment."],
  ["what does idk mean | idk meaning", "**IDK** means *I don't know*."],
  ["what does smh mean | smh meaning", "**SMH** means *shaking my head* — showing disbelief or mild disappointment. Example: *He forgot his shoes again, smh.*"],
  ["what does tbh mean | tbh meaning", "**TBH** means *to be honest*. Example: *TBH, I liked the book more than the movie.*"],
  ["what does omg mean | omg meaning", "**OMG** means *oh my gosh* (or *oh my god*) — showing surprise or excitement."],
  ["what does btw mean | btw meaning", "**BTW** means *by the way* — used to add an extra thought. Example: *BTW, practice starts early tomorrow.*"],
  ["what does imo mean | what does imho mean | imo meaning", "**IMO** means *in my opinion*, and **IMHO** means *in my humble (or honest) opinion*. Example: *IMO, pizza is the best food.*"],
  ["what does ttyl mean | ttyl meaning", "**TTYL** means *talk to you later*."],
  ["what does fyi mean | fyi meaning", "**FYI** means *for your information* — sharing something useful. Example: *FYI, the library closes at 5 today.*"],
  ["what does asap mean | asap meaning", "**ASAP** means *as soon as possible*."],
  ["what does goat mean | goat meaning slang | what does goated mean", "In slang, **GOAT** means *Greatest Of All Time*. Calling someone *goated* means they're amazing at something."],
  ["what does irl mean | irl meaning", "**IRL** means *in real life* — as opposed to online. Example: *We met online but finally hung out IRL.*"],
  ["what does np mean | what does ty mean | ty np meaning", "**TY** means *thank you* and **NP** means *no problem*. You'll often see them together: *TY! — NP!*"],
  ["what does jk mean | jk meaning", "**JK** means *just kidding* — showing you were joking."],
  ["what does afk mean | afk meaning", "**AFK** means *away from keyboard* — common in gaming when someone steps away."],
  ["what does gg mean | gg meaning", "**GG** means *good game* — a friendly thing to say at the end of a match, win or lose."],
  ["what does rofl mean | rofl meaning", "**ROFL** means *rolling on the floor laughing* — something is extremely funny."],
  ["what does nvm mean | nvm meaning", "**NVM** means *never mind*."],
  ["what does ikr mean | ikr meaning", "**IKR** means *I know, right?* — agreeing enthusiastically."],
  ["what does tl;dr mean | tldr meaning | what does tldr mean", "**TL;DR** means *too long; didn't read* — it introduces a short summary of a long message. Example: *TL;DR: the trip is moved to Friday.*"],
  ["what does dm mean | dm meaning", "**DM** means *direct message* — a private message sent to one person on an app or social site."],
  ["what does pov mean | pov meaning", "**POV** means *point of view* — how something looks from a particular person's perspective."],
  ["what does vibe mean | vibe meaning slang | what does vibing mean", "A **vibe** is the feeling or atmosphere of a person, place or thing. *Vibing* means relaxing and enjoying the moment. Example: *This café has a cozy vibe.*"],
  ["what does lowkey mean | lowkey meaning | what does highkey mean", "**Lowkey** means *a little* or *quietly/secretly*: *I lowkey want pizza.* **Highkey** means *very much* or *openly*: *I highkey love this song.*"],
  ["what does sus mean | sus meaning slang", "**Sus** is short for *suspicious* — something seems a bit off. Example: *That excuse sounds kind of sus.*"],
  ["what does no cap mean | cap meaning slang", "**No cap** means *no lie* or *for real*. *Cap* means a lie. Example: *That was the best burger ever, no cap.*"],
  ["what does ambiguous mean | define ambiguous | meaning of ambiguous", "**Ambiguous** means unclear because it could have more than one meaning. Example: *\"I saw her duck\" is ambiguous — a bird, or ducking down?*"],
  ["what does benevolent mean | define benevolent | meaning of benevolent", "**Benevolent** means kind and wanting to do good for others. Example: *The benevolent king shared food with everyone.*"],
  ["what does candid mean | define candid | meaning of candid", "**Candid** means honest and direct. A *candid photo* is one taken naturally, without posing. Example: *She gave a candid answer.*"],
  ["what does diligent mean | define diligent | meaning of diligent", "**Diligent** means hardworking and careful. Example: *A diligent student checks every answer.*"],
  ["what does eloquent mean | define eloquent | meaning of eloquent", "**Eloquent** means able to speak or write clearly and beautifully. Example: *Her eloquent speech moved the whole audience.*"],
  ["what does ephemeral mean | define ephemeral | meaning of ephemeral", "**Ephemeral** means lasting only a very short time. Example: *A rainbow is ephemeral — it fades within minutes.*"],
  ["what does gregarious mean | define gregarious | meaning of gregarious", "**Gregarious** means sociable and liking to be with others. Example: *Our gregarious neighbor knows everyone on the street.*"],
  ["what does meticulous mean | define meticulous | meaning of meticulous", "**Meticulous** means extremely careful about details. Example: *He was meticulous about organizing his stamp collection.*"],
  ["what does nostalgia mean | define nostalgia | meaning of nostalgic | what is nostalgia", "**Nostalgia** is a warm, slightly wistful longing for the past. Example: *Old cartoons fill my dad with nostalgia.*"],
  ["what does obsolete mean | define obsolete | meaning of obsolete", "**Obsolete** means no longer used because something newer replaced it. Example: *Floppy disks are now obsolete.*"],
  ["what does paradox mean | define paradox | meaning of paradox | what is a paradox", "A **paradox** is a statement that seems to contradict itself but may still be true. Example: *\"Less is more.\"*"],
  ["what does pragmatic mean | define pragmatic | meaning of pragmatic", "**Pragmatic** means practical and focused on what actually works. Example: *Her pragmatic plan saved both time and money.*"],
  ["what does serendipity mean | define serendipity | meaning of serendipity", "**Serendipity** is a happy accident — finding something good without looking for it. Example: *Meeting my best friend on a delayed train was pure serendipity.*"],
  ["what does tenacious mean | define tenacious | meaning of tenacious", "**Tenacious** means determined and not giving up easily. Example: *The tenacious climber reached the summit on her third try.*"],
  ["what does vivid mean | define vivid | meaning of vivid", "**Vivid** means bright, strong or very clear. Example: *She had a vivid dream about flying over the ocean.*"],
  ["what does zealous mean | define zealous | meaning of zealous", "**Zealous** means full of energetic enthusiasm for something. Example: *The zealous fans cheered for hours.*"],
  ["what does integrity mean | define integrity | meaning of integrity", "**Integrity** means being honest and doing the right thing, even when no one is watching. Example: *She showed integrity by returning the lost wallet.*"],
  ["what does perseverance mean | define perseverance | meaning of persevere", "**Perseverance** means continuing to try even when something is difficult. Example: *Learning piano takes perseverance.*"],
  ["what does gratitude mean | define gratitude | meaning of grateful", "**Gratitude** is the feeling of being thankful. Example: *I wrote a note to show my gratitude for her help.*"],
  ["what does compassion mean | define compassion | meaning of compassionate", "**Compassion** is caring about others' suffering and wanting to help. Example: *The nurse treated every patient with compassion.*"],
  ["what does anxiety mean | define anxiety | meaning of anxious", "**Anxiety** is a feeling of worry or nervousness about what might happen. Example: *He felt some anxiety before his first speech.* Talking to someone you trust can help."],
  ["what does sarcasm mean | define sarcasm | meaning of sarcastic | what is sarcasm", "**Sarcasm** is saying the opposite of what you mean, usually to tease or mock. Example: *\"Oh great, more homework,\" he said sarcastically.*"],
  ["what does inevitable mean | define inevitable | meaning of inevitable", "**Inevitable** means certain to happen; impossible to avoid. Example: *If you don't water a plant, wilting is inevitable.*"],
  ["what does ironic mean | meaning of ironic | define ironic", "**Ironic** describes something that's the opposite of what you'd expect. Example: *It's ironic that the fire station burned down.*"],
  ["what does mundane mean | define mundane | meaning of mundane", "**Mundane** means ordinary or dull. Example: *Folding laundry is a mundane chore.*"],
  ["what does cryptic mean | define cryptic | meaning of cryptic", "**Cryptic** means mysterious and hard to understand. Example: *He left a cryptic note that said only, \"Look under the stairs.\"*"],
  ["what does reluctant mean | define reluctant | meaning of reluctant", "**Reluctant** means unwilling or hesitant. Example: *The cat was reluctant to take a bath.*"],
  ["what does skeptical mean | define skeptical | meaning of skeptic", "**Skeptical** means doubtful and not easily convinced. Example: *I was skeptical that the tiny tent would fit four people.*"],
  ["what does spontaneous mean | define spontaneous | meaning of spontaneous", "**Spontaneous** means done suddenly, without planning. Example: *We took a spontaneous trip to the beach.*"],
  ["what does innovative mean | define innovative | meaning of innovation", "**Innovative** means new, creative and original. Example: *The innovative design made the bike fold into a backpack.*"],
  ["what does sustainable mean | define sustainable | meaning of sustainability", "**Sustainable** means able to continue for a long time without using up resources or harming the environment. Example: *Solar power is a sustainable energy source.*"],
  ["what does hypothesis mean | define hypothesis | meaning of hypothesis", "A **hypothesis** is an educated guess you can test. Example: *My hypothesis is that plants grow faster with more sunlight.*"],
  ["what does inference mean | define inference | meaning of infer", "An **inference** is a conclusion you reach from clues and evidence. To **infer** is to figure something out. Example: *His wet umbrella let me infer it was raining.*"],
  ["what does imply mean | imply vs infer | difference between imply and infer", "The speaker **implies** (hints at something); the listener **infers** (figures it out). Example: *She implied she was tired by yawning; I inferred she wanted to go home.*"],
  ["what does mediocre mean | define mediocre | meaning of mediocre", "**Mediocre** means only average, not very good. Example: *The movie was mediocre — not bad, not great.*"],
  ["what does feasible mean | define feasible | meaning of feasible", "**Feasible** means possible and practical to do. Example: *Walking to school is feasible since it's only a mile away.*"],
  ["what does lethargic mean | define lethargic | meaning of lethargic", "**Lethargic** means sluggish and lacking energy. Example: *I felt lethargic after staying up late.*"],
  ["what does nonchalant mean | define nonchalant | meaning of nonchalant", "**Nonchalant** means calm and relaxed, seeming not to care. Example: *He acted nonchalant, but he was secretly excited.*"],
  ["what does quintessential mean | define quintessential | meaning of quintessential", "**Quintessential** means the most perfect or typical example of something. Example: *Apple pie is the quintessential American dessert.*"],
  ["what does facetious mean | define facetious | meaning of facetious", "**Facetious** means joking at an inappropriate time, not meant seriously. Example: *Don't be facetious — this is important.*"],
  ["what does juxtaposition mean | define juxtaposition | meaning of juxtapose", "**Juxtaposition** is placing two different things side by side to highlight their contrast. Example: *The tiny puppy next to the huge horse made a funny juxtaposition.*"],
  ["what does euphoria mean | define euphoria | meaning of euphoric", "**Euphoria** is a feeling of intense happiness and excitement. Example: *The team felt euphoria after winning the championship.*"],
  ["what does melancholy mean | define melancholy | meaning of melancholy", "**Melancholy** is a deep, quiet sadness. Example: *The gray, rainy day gave the town a melancholy feel.*"],
  ["what does altruism mean | define altruism | meaning of altruistic", "**Altruism** is caring about others and helping them without expecting anything back. Example: *Volunteering at the shelter was an act of altruism.*"],
  ["what does pessimist mean | optimist vs pessimist | define optimist", "An **optimist** expects good things to happen (*the glass is half full*). A **pessimist** expects the worst (*the glass is half empty*)."],
  ["what does introvert mean | introvert vs extrovert | define extrovert", "An **introvert** recharges by spending quiet time alone; an **extrovert** recharges by being around people. Many people are a mix — sometimes called an *ambivert*."],
  ["what does cynical mean | define cynical | meaning of cynic", "**Cynical** means believing people are mostly selfish or that things won't turn out well. Example: *He was cynical about the free offer, suspecting a catch.*"],
  ["what does verbose mean | define verbose | meaning of verbose", "**Verbose** means using more words than needed. Example: *The verbose instructions took two pages to explain one step.*"],
  ["what does concise mean | define concise | meaning of concise", "**Concise** means short and clear, with no extra words. Example: *Her concise summary fit in one sentence.*"],
  ["what does plethora mean | define plethora | meaning of plethora", "A **plethora** is a large amount — often more than enough. Example: *The buffet had a plethora of desserts.*"],
  ["what does ominous mean | define ominous | meaning of ominous", "**Ominous** means giving the feeling that something bad is about to happen. Example: *Ominous dark clouds rolled in before the storm.*"],
  ["what does profound mean | define profound | meaning of profound", "**Profound** means very deep, meaningful or intense. Example: *The book had a profound effect on how I see the world.*"],
  ["what does arbitrary mean | define arbitrary | meaning of arbitrary", "**Arbitrary** means based on random choice rather than a reason or rule. Example: *The order of the list was arbitrary.*"],
  ["what does catastrophe mean | define catastrophe | meaning of catastrophic", "A **catastrophe** is a terrible disaster. Example: *The flood was a catastrophe for the small village.*"],
  ["what does dilemma mean | define dilemma | meaning of dilemma", "A **dilemma** is a hard choice between two options, often both difficult. Example: *My dilemma: go to the party or finish my project?*"],
  ["what does enigma mean | define enigma | meaning of enigmatic", "An **enigma** is a mystery or puzzle that's hard to figure out. Example: *How the cat got onto the roof remains an enigma.*"],
  ["what does frugal mean | define frugal | meaning of frugal", "**Frugal** means careful with money and avoiding waste. Example: *Being frugal helped her save for a new bike.*"],
  ["what does humble mean | define humble | meaning of humility", "**Humble** means modest and not boastful about yourself. Example: *Even after winning, she stayed humble and thanked her team.*"],
  ["what does inquisitive mean | define inquisitive | meaning of inquisitive", "**Inquisitive** means curious and eager to learn or ask questions. Example: *The inquisitive child asked why the sky is blue.*"],
  ["what does jubilant mean | define jubilant | meaning of jubilant", "**Jubilant** means joyful and celebrating. Example: *The jubilant crowd danced in the streets.*"],
  ["what does kudos mean | kudos meaning", "**Kudos** means praise or credit for an achievement. Example: *Kudos to the chef for an amazing meal!*"],
  ["what does luminous mean | define luminous | meaning of luminous", "**Luminous** means glowing or giving off light. Example: *The luminous moon lit up the path.*"],
  ["what does mitigate mean | define mitigate | meaning of mitigate", "To **mitigate** means to make something less bad or severe. Example: *Wearing a helmet mitigates the risk of injury.*"],
  ["what does novice mean | define novice | meaning of novice", "A **novice** is a beginner. Example: *As a novice skater, I spent a lot of time on the ice — sitting.*"],
  ["what does oblivious mean | define oblivious | meaning of oblivious", "**Oblivious** means not aware of what's happening around you. Example: *He was oblivious to the dog stealing his sandwich.*"],
  ["what does pristine mean | define pristine | meaning of pristine", "**Pristine** means perfectly clean, fresh or untouched. Example: *The new sneakers were pristine white.*"],
  ["what does redundant mean | define redundant | meaning of redundant", "**Redundant** means unnecessary because it repeats something. Example: *\"Free gift\" is redundant — gifts are already free.*"],
  ["what does tedious mean | define tedious | meaning of tedious", "**Tedious** means long, slow and boring. Example: *Sorting a thousand buttons by color was tedious.*"],
  ["what does unanimous mean | define unanimous | meaning of unanimous", "**Unanimous** means everyone agrees. Example: *The vote for pizza night was unanimous.*"],
  ["what does versatile mean | define versatile | meaning of versatile", "**Versatile** means able to do many different things well. Example: *A pocketknife is a versatile tool.*"],
  ["what does wanderlust mean | define wanderlust | meaning of wanderlust", "**Wanderlust** is a strong desire to travel and explore the world. Example: *Her wanderlust took her to twelve countries.*"],
  ["what does xenophobia mean | define xenophobia | meaning of xenophobia", "**Xenophobia** is fear or dislike of people from other countries. It's the opposite of being welcoming and open-minded."],
  ["what does yearn mean | define yearn | meaning of yearning", "To **yearn** is to want something very deeply. Example: *After a long trip, I yearned for my own bed.*"],
  ["what does zenith mean | define zenith | meaning of zenith", "The **zenith** is the highest point — of the sky, or of someone's success. Example: *At noon the sun was near its zenith.*"],
  ["what does aesthetic mean | define aesthetic | aesthetic meaning slang", "**Aesthetic** relates to beauty and how something looks. As slang, it means a particular visual style or vibe. Example: *Her room has a cozy, plant-filled aesthetic.*"],
  ["what does cringe mean | cringe meaning slang", "To **cringe** is to wince with embarrassment. As slang, *cringe* describes something awkward or embarrassing to watch. Example: *That old video of me singing is so cringe.*"],
  ["what does bae mean | what does bestie mean | bestie meaning", "**Bestie** means *best friend*. **Bae** is a sweet nickname for someone you're close to, often a partner."],
  ["what does salty mean slang | salty meaning", "In slang, being **salty** means being bitter or annoyed, often over something small. Example: *He's still salty about losing the board game.*"],
  ["what does ghosting mean | ghosted meaning", "**Ghosting** is suddenly stopping all contact with someone without explanation. Example: *She ghosted the group chat for a week.*"],
  ["what does slay mean slang | slay meaning", "In slang, to **slay** means to do something amazingly well. Example: *You slayed that dance performance!*"],
  ["what does bet mean slang | bet meaning in texting", "In slang, **bet** means *okay*, *sure* or *deal*. Example: *Meet at 3? — Bet.*"],
  ["what does mid mean slang | mid meaning", "In slang, **mid** means average or just okay — not great. Example: *The sequel was kind of mid.*"],
  ["what does rizz mean | rizz meaning", "**Rizz** is slang for charm or charisma — the ability to win people over. It comes from the middle of the word *charisma*."],
  ["what does periodt mean | period slang meaning", "**Periodt** (or *period*) at the end of a statement means *end of discussion — that's final*. Example: *Pizza is the best food, periodt.*"],
  ["how do you say hello in spanish | how to say thank you in spanish | basic spanish words | spanish for goodbye", "**Spanish** basics:\n• Hello — *hola* (OH-lah)\n• Thank you — *gracias* (GRAH-see-ahs)\n• Goodbye — *adiós* (ah-dee-OHS)\n• Please — *por favor*\n• Yes — *sí*\n• No — *no*"],
  ["how do you say hello in french | how to say thank you in french | basic french words | french for goodbye", "**French** basics:\n• Hello — *bonjour* (bohn-ZHOOR)\n• Thank you — *merci* (mair-SEE)\n• Goodbye — *au revoir* (oh ruh-VWAHR)\n• Please — *s'il vous plaît* (seel voo PLEH)\n• Yes — *oui* (wee)\n• No — *non*"],
  ["how do you say hello in german | how to say thank you in german | basic german words | german for goodbye", "**German** basics:\n• Hello — *hallo* or *guten Tag*\n• Thank you — *danke* (DAHN-kuh)\n• Goodbye — *auf Wiedersehen* (formal) or *tschüss* (casual)\n• Please — *bitte* (BIT-uh)\n• Yes — *ja* (yah)\n• No — *nein* (nine)"],
  ["how do you say hello in italian | how to say thank you in italian | basic italian words | italian for goodbye", "**Italian** basics:\n• Hello — *ciao* (chow) or *buongiorno*\n• Thank you — *grazie* (GRAHT-see-eh)\n• Goodbye — *arrivederci* (ah-ree-veh-DEHR-chee)\n• Please — *per favore*\n• Yes — *sì*\n• No — *no*"],
  ["how do you say hello in portuguese | how to say thank you in portuguese | basic portuguese words | portuguese for goodbye", "**Portuguese** basics:\n• Hello — *olá*\n• Thank you — *obrigado* (if you're male) / *obrigada* (if you're female)\n• Goodbye — *adeus* or casual *tchau*\n• Please — *por favor*\n• Yes — *sim*\n• No — *não* (nown)"],
  ["how do you say hello in japanese | how to say thank you in japanese | basic japanese words | japanese for goodbye", "**Japanese** basics:\n• Hello — *konnichiwa* (こんにちは)\n• Thank you — *arigatō* (ありがとう)\n• Goodbye — *sayōnara* (さようなら)\n• Please — *onegaishimasu* (お願いします)\n• Yes — *hai* (はい)\n• No — *iie* (いいえ, ee-EH)"],
  ["how do you say hello in chinese | how to say thank you in mandarin | basic mandarin chinese words | chinese for goodbye", "**Mandarin Chinese** basics:\n• Hello — *nǐ hǎo* (你好)\n• Thank you — *xièxie* (谢谢, shyeh-shyeh)\n• Goodbye — *zàijiàn* (再见, dzai-jyen)\n• Please — *qǐng* (请, ching)\n• Yes — *shì* (是) or *duì* (对, *correct*)\n• No — *bù* (不) or *bú shì* (不是)"],
  ["how do you say hello in korean | how to say thank you in korean | basic korean words | korean for goodbye", "**Korean** basics:\n• Hello — *annyeonghaseyo* (안녕하세요)\n• Thank you — *gamsahamnida* (감사합니다)\n• Goodbye — *annyeonghi gaseyo* (안녕히 가세요, to someone leaving)\n• Please — *juseyo* (주세요, when asking for something)\n• Yes — *ne* (네)\n• No — *aniyo* (아니요)"],
  ["how do you say hello in hindi | how to say thank you in hindi | basic hindi words | hindi for goodbye", "**Hindi** basics:\n• Hello — *namaste* (नमस्ते)\n• Thank you — *dhanyavaad* (धन्यवाद) or *shukriya*\n• Goodbye — *alvida* (अलविदा) or *phir milenge* (see you again)\n• Please — *kripya* (कृपया)\n• Yes — *haan* (हाँ)\n• No — *nahin* (नहीं)"],
  ["how do you say hello in arabic | how to say thank you in arabic | basic arabic words | arabic for goodbye", "**Arabic** basics:\n• Hello — *marhaban* (مرحبا) or *as-salamu alaykum* (peace be upon you)\n• Thank you — *shukran* (شكرا)\n• Goodbye — *ma'a as-salama* (مع السلامة)\n• Please — *min fadlak* (to a man) / *min fadlik* (to a woman)\n• Yes — *na'am* (نعم)\n• No — *la* (لا)"],
  ["how do you say hello in russian | how to say thank you in russian | basic russian words | russian for goodbye", "**Russian** basics:\n• Hello — *privet* (привет, casual) or *zdravstvuyte* (здравствуйте, polite)\n• Thank you — *spasibo* (спасибо)\n• Goodbye — *do svidaniya* (до свидания)\n• Please — *pozhaluysta* (пожалуйста)\n• Yes — *da* (да)\n• No — *net* (нет)"],
  ["how do you say hello in dutch | how to say thank you in dutch | basic dutch words | dutch for goodbye", "**Dutch** basics:\n• Hello — *hallo* or *hoi*\n• Thank you — *dank je wel* (casual) or *dank u wel* (polite)\n• Goodbye — *tot ziens* or casual *doei*\n• Please — *alsjeblieft* (casual) / *alstublieft* (polite)\n• Yes — *ja*\n• No — *nee*"],
  ["how do you say hello in swahili | how to say thank you in swahili | basic swahili words | swahili for goodbye", "**Swahili** basics:\n• Hello — *jambo* or *habari* (how are you?)\n• Thank you — *asante* (ah-SAHN-teh)\n• Goodbye — *kwaheri*\n• Please — *tafadhali*\n• Yes — *ndiyo*\n• No — *hapana*"],
  ["how do you say hello in hebrew | how to say thank you in hebrew | basic hebrew words | hebrew for goodbye", "**Hebrew** basics:\n• Hello — *shalom* (שלום, also means *peace*)\n• Thank you — *todah* (תודה)\n• Goodbye — *lehitra'ot* (להתראות, see you) or *shalom*\n• Please — *bevakasha* (בבקשה)\n• Yes — *ken* (כן)\n• No — *lo* (לא)"],
  ["how do you say hello in greek | how to say thank you in greek | basic greek words | greek for goodbye", "**Greek** basics:\n• Hello — *yia sou* (Γεια σου, YAH-soo)\n• Thank you — *efcharistó* (ευχαριστώ, ef-hah-ree-STOH)\n• Goodbye — *antío* (αντίο)\n• Please — *parakaló* (παρακαλώ)\n• Yes — *nai* (ναι, sounds like *neh*!)\n• No — *óchi* (όχι, OH-hee)"],
  ["how do you say hello in different languages | hello in other languages | say hello in many languages", "Hello around the world: *hola* (Spanish), *bonjour* (French), *hallo* (German), *ciao* (Italian), *olá* (Portuguese), *konnichiwa* (Japanese), *nǐ hǎo* (Mandarin), *annyeonghaseyo* (Korean), *namaste* (Hindi), *marhaban* (Arabic), *privet* (Russian), *jambo* (Swahili), *shalom* (Hebrew), *yia sou* (Greek)."],
  ["how do you say thank you in different languages | thank you in other languages", "Thank you around the world: *gracias* (Spanish), *merci* (French), *danke* (German), *grazie* (Italian), *obrigado/a* (Portuguese), *arigatō* (Japanese), *xièxie* (Mandarin), *gamsahamnida* (Korean), *dhanyavaad* (Hindi), *shukran* (Arabic), *spasibo* (Russian), *asante* (Swahili), *todah* (Hebrew), *efcharistó* (Greek)."],
  ["how do you say i love you in different languages | i love you in other languages | how to say i love you in spanish", "*I love you* around the world: *te quiero / te amo* (Spanish), *je t'aime* (French), *ich liebe dich* (German), *ti amo* (Italian), *eu te amo* (Portuguese), *wǒ ài nǐ* (Mandarin), *saranghae* (Korean), *aishiteru* (Japanese)."],
  ["what is the most spoken language in the world | most popular language", "By total speakers (native plus learners), **English** is the most spoken language. By *native* speakers, **Mandarin Chinese** is number one, followed by Spanish."],
  ["how many languages are there in the world | how many languages exist", "There are roughly **7,000 languages** spoken in the world today. Many are spoken by small communities, and some are endangered."],
  ["what is the hardest language to learn | hardest language for english speakers", "For English speakers, languages like **Mandarin**, **Japanese**, **Korean** and **Arabic** are often considered the hardest, because of new writing systems, tones or grammar. Languages like Spanish, Dutch and Norwegian are usually easier."],
  ["how do i learn a new language | tips for learning a language | best way to learn a language", "Practice a little every day, learn the most common words first, listen to music and shows in that language, speak out loud (even to yourself!), and don't fear mistakes — they're how you learn."],
  ["what language do they speak in brazil | do brazilians speak spanish", "People in **Brazil** speak **Portuguese**, not Spanish — it's the largest Portuguese-speaking country in the world."],
  ["what is sign language | is sign language universal", "**Sign language** uses hand shapes, movements and facial expressions to communicate. It's not universal: there's American Sign Language (ASL), British Sign Language (BSL) and many others, each with its own grammar."],
  ["how to write an essay | essay writing steps | how do i write an essay | essay structure", "A basic essay has three parts:\n1. **Introduction** — hook the reader and state your thesis (main point).\n2. **Body paragraphs** — each covers one supporting idea with evidence and explanation.\n3. **Conclusion** — restate your thesis in new words and leave a final thought.\nPlan first, write a draft, then revise and proofread."],
  ["what is a five paragraph essay | 5 paragraph essay format", "A **five-paragraph essay** has an introduction with a thesis, three body paragraphs (each with one main reason and evidence), and a conclusion that sums up and wraps things up. It's a great starter structure."],
  ["how to write an introduction | how to start an essay | how to write a good intro", "Start with a **hook** (a question, surprising fact or short story), give a little **background**, then end with your **thesis statement**. Example: *Have you ever wondered why cats purr? ... Cats purr to communicate, to calm themselves and even to heal.*"],
  ["how to write a conclusion | how to end an essay | how to write a good conclusion", "In a **conclusion**, restate your thesis in fresh words, briefly sum up your main points, and end with a strong final thought — a call to action, a question, or why it matters. Don't add brand-new evidence."],
  ["what is a thesis statement | how to write a thesis | thesis statement example", "A **thesis statement** is one sentence that states your essay's main argument. It should be specific and arguable. Example: *Schools should start later because teens need more sleep, learn better, and are safer on the road.*"],
  ["what is a hook in writing | hook sentence examples | how to write a hook", "A **hook** is an opening line that grabs attention. Try a surprising fact (*Octopuses have three hearts.*), a question (*What would you do with a free day?*), a quote, or a vivid scene."],
  ["what is a body paragraph | how to write a body paragraph", "A **body paragraph** supports your thesis with one main point. A handy pattern: **topic sentence** → **evidence** (fact, example, quote) → **explanation** of how it proves your point → **transition** to the next idea."],
  ["what are transition words | transition words examples | words to connect paragraphs", "**Transition words** link ideas smoothly. To add: *also, furthermore*. To contrast: *however, on the other hand*. To show order: *first, next, finally*. To conclude: *in conclusion, overall*."],
  ["how to write a story | how do i write a short story | tips for writing a story", "Start with a **character** who wants something, give them a **problem** that gets in the way, build up to a **climax**, then show how it's **resolved**. Use vivid details, some dialogue, and *show, don't tell*."],
  ["what does show don't tell mean | show don't tell examples", "**Show, don't tell** means letting readers see emotions through actions and details instead of just naming them. *Tell*: *She was nervous.* *Show*: *She chewed her pencil and kept glancing at the clock.*"],
  ["how to come up with story ideas | i need a story idea | give me a story prompt", "Try a **what if** question: *What if your shadow ran away? What if a library book let you step inside it? What if your pet could talk — but only told lies?* Pick one and ask: who wants what, and what's stopping them?"],
  ["how to write a poem | how do i write poetry | tips for writing a poem", "Pick a topic you care about, focus on one feeling or moment, and use your senses to describe it. Play with line breaks, rhythm and comparisons (similes and metaphors). Poems don't have to rhyme — read yours aloud and trim any word that doesn't earn its place."],
  ["how to write a letter | how do i write a letter | letter format", "A letter usually has: the **date**, a **greeting** (*Dear Grandma,*), the **body** (your message in paragraphs), a **closing** (*Love,* or *Sincerely,*) and your **signature**. Formal letters also include addresses at the top."],
  ["how to write a formal letter | formal letter format | business letter format", "A **formal letter**: your address and the date at the top, then the recipient's address, a greeting (*Dear Ms. Lee:*), a clear purpose in the first line, short polite paragraphs, a closing like *Sincerely,* and your full name."],
  ["how to write a thank you note | thank you letter example | how to write a thank you card", "Keep it warm and specific: thank them for the exact gift or kindness, say how you'll use it or how it made you feel, and close kindly. Example: *Dear Aunt Jo, thank you for the art set! I've already painted three sunsets with it. Love, Mia.*"],
  ["how to write an email | how to write a professional email | email format", "A good email has a clear **subject line**, a greeting (*Hi Mr. Patel,*), a short first line that says why you're writing, the key details, a polite closing (*Thanks,* or *Best,*) and your name. Keep it brief and proofread before sending."],
  ["how to write an email to a teacher | email to teacher example", "Example:\n*Subject: Question about the science project*\n*Hi Ms. Rivera,*\n*I'm in your 3rd period class. Could you tell me if the project can be a poster instead of a slideshow? Thank you for your help!*\n*Best,*\n*Jordan*"],
  ["how to write a persuasive paragraph | persuasive writing tips | how to persuade in writing", "State your **opinion** clearly, give **reasons** backed by facts or examples, answer an **objection** (*Some say... but...*), and end with a **call to action**. Example: *Our school should have a garden. It teaches science, provides fresh food, and gives students a calm place to relax.*"],
  ["what is persuasive writing | what is an argumentative essay | persuasive vs argumentative", "**Persuasive writing** tries to convince the reader to agree or act, often using emotion and strong reasons. An **argumentative essay** is more formal, relies on evidence, and fairly addresses the other side before showing why its view is stronger."],
  ["how to write a summary | how do i summarize | summary writing tips", "To **summarize**, read the text, find the main idea and the most important points, then retell them briefly **in your own words** — no small details or personal opinions. A good summary is usually much shorter than the original."],
  ["how to write a book report | book report format | book report outline", "A **book report** usually includes: the title and author, the main characters, the setting, a short plot summary (no need to tell every detail), the theme or lesson, and your opinion — what you liked and whether you'd recommend it."],
  ["how to write a paragraph | how do i write a good paragraph", "Start with a **topic sentence** that states the main idea, add 3–5 **supporting sentences** with details or examples, and end with a **concluding sentence** that wraps it up or leads to the next idea."],
  ["how to improve my writing | tips to improve writing | how to become a better writer", "Read a lot, write a little every day, use specific nouns and strong verbs, cut unnecessary words, vary your sentence length, read your work aloud, and always revise — great writing is rewriting."],
  ["how to make my writing more descriptive | how to describe things in writing", "Use your **five senses**, swap general words for specific ones (*dog → shaggy golden retriever*), choose strong verbs (*walked → tiptoed*), and add comparisons. Example: *The kitchen smelled like warm cinnamon and burnt toast.*"],
  ["how to proofread | proofreading tips | how to check my writing for mistakes", "Take a break first, then read slowly and **out loud**. Check one thing at a time — spelling, then punctuation, then grammar. Reading backwards sentence by sentence helps catch typos. Spell-check helps but won't catch *their/there* mix-ups."],
  ["what is writer's block | how to beat writer's block | i can't think of what to write", "**Writer's block** is getting stuck. Try freewriting for 5 minutes without stopping, start in the middle instead of the beginning, make a quick list of ideas, change where you're writing, or take a short walk and come back."],
  ["how to write an outline | essay outline example", "An **outline** is a plan. List your thesis at the top, then use Roman numerals for main sections (*I. Intro, II. Reason 1, III. Reason 2...*) and letters for supporting details beneath each one."],
  ["what is a rough draft | what is revising vs editing | difference between revising and editing", "A **rough draft** is your first try — it doesn't need to be perfect. **Revising** improves big things (ideas, order, clarity); **editing** fixes small things (spelling, grammar, punctuation)."],
  ["how to write a speech | tips for writing a speech", "Open with a hook, make two or three clear main points, use stories or examples people will remember, keep sentences short and easy to say, and end with a memorable line. Practice it out loud several times."],
  ["how to write a biography | how to write about a person", "Research the person's life, then write in order: their early life, important events and achievements, challenges they faced, and why they matter today. Include interesting facts and cite where you found them."],
  ["how to write a news article | news article format", "Start with a catchy **headline**, then a **lead** sentence that answers *who, what, when, where, why and how*. Put the most important facts first and less important details later. Stick to facts and quotes, not opinions."],
  ["how to write a diary entry | how to write a journal entry", "Start with the date, write in first person (*I*), and describe what happened and how you felt about it. There are no rules — it's your private space to think, dream and remember."],
  ["how to cite a source | what is a citation | how to write a bibliography", "A **citation** credits where you found information. A simple one includes the author, title, publisher or website, and date. A **bibliography** lists all your sources at the end, usually in alphabetical order by author."],
  ["what is plagiarism | is copying writing plagiarism", "**Plagiarism** is using someone else's words or ideas as if they were your own. Avoid it by writing in your own words and giving credit (citing) when you use someone's work or quote them."],
  ["how to write a haiku | haiku writing tips", "Pick a single moment in nature, then write three lines of **5, 7 and 5 syllables**. Count on your fingers! Focus on what you see, hear or feel right now, and try to end with a small surprise or shift."],
  ["how to write a limerick | limerick writing tips", "Write five lines. Lines 1, 2 and 5 are longer and rhyme (A); lines 3 and 4 are shorter and rhyme with each other (B). Start with *There once was a ___ from ___* and make the ending silly."],
  ["how many words is a paragraph | how many words in an essay | how long should an essay be", "A typical paragraph is about **100–200 words**. A short school essay is often **500–800 words**, while a five-paragraph essay usually lands around 500. Always check your assignment's requirements."],
  ["what is a good word for said | other words for said | synonyms for said", "Instead of *said*, try: *whispered, shouted, asked, replied, muttered, exclaimed, giggled, sighed, explained, insisted, called* or *mumbled* — but plain *said* is fine much of the time!"],
  ["other words for happy | synonyms for happy | another word for happy", "Synonyms for **happy**: *joyful, cheerful, glad, delighted, content, thrilled, ecstatic, merry, upbeat, overjoyed.*"],
  ["other words for sad | synonyms for sad | another word for sad", "Synonyms for **sad**: *unhappy, gloomy, down, blue, miserable, heartbroken, sorrowful, glum, downcast, melancholy.*"],
  ["other words for good | synonyms for good | another word for good", "Synonyms for **good**: *great, excellent, wonderful, fine, terrific, superb, pleasant, outstanding, solid, splendid.*"],
  ["other words for big | synonyms for big | another word for large", "Synonyms for **big**: *large, huge, enormous, giant, massive, gigantic, vast, colossal, immense, towering.*"],
  ["other words for very | how to avoid using very | instead of very", "Replace *very + word* with one stronger word: *very tired → exhausted*, *very big → enormous*, *very happy → thrilled*, *very scared → terrified*, *very cold → freezing*."],
  ["write a poem about the ocean | ocean poem | poem about the sea | write me a sea poem", "**The Ocean**\n\nThe ocean hums a silver tune,\nit rocks the ships, it greets the moon.\nIt hides the whales in rooms of blue\nand scatters shells for me and you.\n\nIt pulls the shore, then lets it go,\nlike breathing slowly, to and fro —\na giant heart that never sleeps,\nwith secrets folded in its deeps."],
  ["write a short story about a dragon | dragon story | tell me a story about a dragon", "**The Dragon Who Couldn't Roar**\n\nPip was the smallest dragon in the mountain, and when he tried to roar, only bubbles came out. The other dragons laughed. One winter, the village below lost its fire in a blizzard, and the big dragons' roars only blew the snow around. Pip flew down, took a deep breath, and gently blew a warm, glowing bubble that floated into every chimney. By morning, every home was cozy. Nobody laughed at Pip again — they asked him to teach them."],
  ["write a haiku about cats | cat haiku | haiku about a cat", "*Sunbeam on the rug —*\n*a cat melts into its warmth,*\n*one ear stays awake.*"],
  ["write a haiku about rain | rain haiku", "*Rain taps on the glass;*\n*the puddles practice their songs —*\n*a frog joins the choir.*"],
  ["write a haiku about spring | spring haiku", "*Green buds stretch and yawn;*\n*the old tree remembers how*\n*to bloom all over.*"],
  ["write a haiku about snow | winter haiku | haiku about winter", "*Snowflakes drift and spin,*\n*each one a tiny secret*\n*whispered to the ground.*"],
  ["write a haiku about the moon | moon haiku", "*Silver lantern hangs*\n*above the sleeping rooftops,*\n*lighting dreams for free.*"],
  ["write a haiku about the ocean | ocean haiku | sea haiku", "*Waves fold into foam;*\n*the sea keeps rewriting sand,*\n*then erases it.*"],
  ["write a haiku about dogs | dog haiku | haiku about a dog", "*Muddy paws, wild tail —*\n*he greets me like I have been*\n*gone a hundred years.*"],
  ["write a haiku about autumn | fall haiku | autumn haiku", "*Red leaves let go now,*\n*twirling like tiny dancers —*\n*the wind claps for them.*"],
  ["write a haiku about summer | summer haiku", "*Ice pop drips and drips,*\n*sticky rivers down my wrist —*\n*the sun wins again.*"],
  ["write me a haiku | give me a haiku | can you write a haiku", "*Morning coffee steam*\n*curls like a question mark — what*\n*will today become?*"],
  ["write a poem about friendship | friendship poem | poem about friends", "**A Friend**\n\nA friend is a lamp when the hallway is dark,\na swing that's still warm at the edge of the park,\na laugh that arrives when you've run out of yours,\na key that fits every one of your doors.\n\nSo here is my thanks, though it's simple and small:\nyou make the world bigger — and that's best of all."],
  ["write a poem about space | poem about stars | space poem | write a poem about the stars", "**Up There**\n\nThe stars are freckles on the night,\nthe planets spin in quiet flight.\nThe moon rolls by — a silver wheel —\nand comets streak with icy zeal.\n\nI lie on grass and count each spark,\nand feel so small beneath the dark,\nyet somehow big, because I'm here\nto wonder at the atmosphere."],
  ["write a poem about a cat | cat poem | poem about cats", "**Ode to My Cat**\n\nShe naps in boxes, sinks and shoes,\nshe never asks — she simply chooses.\nShe knocks my pencil off the desk,\nthen yawns as if she needs a rest.\n\nBut when I'm sad, she somehow knows,\nand curls up purring by my toes."],
  ["write a poem about a dog | dog poem | poem about dogs", "**Best Friend in a Fur Coat**\n\nHe wags his tail at every sound,\nhe chases leaves around and round.\nHe thinks the mail carrier's a threat,\nand bath time is his worst regret.\n\nBut when I'm home, he leaps with glee —\nthe happiest dog in history."],
  ["write a poem about rain | rain poem | poem about the rain", "**Rainy Day**\n\nThe rain plays drums on the window sill,\nthe gutters gurgle, the street stands still.\nThe puddles wait like mirrors round,\nfor boots to splash them to the ground.\n\nSo pull your hood and step outside —\nthe sky is giving us a ride!"],
  ["write a poem about nature | nature poem | poem about trees", "**The Tree**\n\nThe tree stands tall with arms held wide,\nit gives the birds a place to hide.\nIt drinks the rain, it eats the sun,\nit shades the kids when games are done.\n\nIt never rushes, never shouts —\nit simply grows, and figures out."],
  ["write a poem about school | school poem | poem about learning", "**Monday Morning**\n\nThe bell rings loud, the hallways hum,\nthe lockers clang like a marching drum.\nWe learn of planets, poems, sums,\nand where the Nile's long water comes.\n\nThough homework piles up like a tower,\nI'm growing smarter every hour."],
  ["write a poem for my mom | poem about mom | mothers day poem", "**For Mom**\n\nYou tied my shoes and dried my tears,\nyou chased away my midnight fears.\nYou cheered the loudest at my game\nand never let me feel ashamed.\n\nSo here's a poem, short and true:\nthe best thing in my world is you."],
  ["write a poem about pizza | pizza poem | funny food poem", "**Pizza Night**\n\nA circle of cheese, a sauce of red,\na crust that's crispy, golden bread.\nI'll take a slice, then maybe two —\nokay, okay, I'll have a few.\n\nSome folks like salads, crisp and green,\nbut pizza is my dinner queen."],
  ["write a funny poem | tell me a funny poem | silly poem", "**My Sock**\n\nI lost a sock inside the dryer,\nI think it's joined a sock-only choir.\nOr maybe it has run away\nto live where all the lost socks play.\n\nIf you should find a sock that's blue,\nplease tell it: *its partner misses you!*"],
  ["write a limerick | tell me a limerick | write a funny limerick", "*There once was a dog named Lou,*\n*Who chewed on his owner's left shoe.*\n*When asked why he ate it,*\n*He said, \"I'd debate it —*\n*But honestly, I was just bored, too!\"*"],
  ["write an acrostic poem | acrostic poem example | write an acrostic poem about music", "**MUSIC**\n*M*elodies drift through the open door,\n*U*p and down like waves on the shore,\n*S*inging the songs my heart adores,\n*I* tap my feet across the floor —\n*C*an't stop now; I want some more!"],
  ["write a poem | write me a poem | can you write a poem | give me a poem", "**Small Things**\n\nA ladybug upon a leaf,\na bit of chalk, a pencil's tip,\nthe first warm sip of cocoa — brief,\nthe way the morning shadows slip.\n\nThe world is full of tiny things\nthat no one stops to see —\nso slow down, friend, and look around:\nthey're waiting there for free."],
  ["write a short story about a robot | robot story | tell me a story about a robot", "**Bolt Learns to Dance**\n\nBolt was built to sort boxes, and he was perfect at it. One day, a radio in the warehouse played a song, and Bolt's foot began to tap. He didn't understand it — tapping wasn't efficient. But every day he hummed a little, and soon he was spinning between the shelves. The workers didn't scold him; they joined in. Bolt still sorted boxes perfectly — he just did it with a twirl."],
  ["write a short story about space | space adventure story | tell me a story about an astronaut", "**The Lonely Star**\n\nCaptain Nova's ship drifted past a tiny star that blinked faintly, as if waving. Curious, she radioed hello. The star blinked back — twice! For weeks she sent it songs and jokes in flashes of light, and it answered in glowing patterns. When she finally headed home, the little star shone a bit brighter than before. Sometimes, all anyone needs is someone to say hello."],
  ["write a story about a lost puppy | puppy story | tell me a story about a puppy", "**Biscuit Finds Home**\n\nBiscuit the puppy chased a butterfly too far and suddenly didn't recognize any street. She sniffed the air — bakery bread, wet grass, and... peanut butter! That smelled like her girl, Lily. Biscuit followed her nose past the park and the library until she heard, *\"Biscuit!\"* Lily scooped her up, and that night Biscuit decided butterflies could wait until tomorrow."],
  ["write a story about magic | magic story | tell me a story about a wizard", "**The Apprentice's Mistake**\n\nTheo the young wizard wanted to make his room clean in one spell. *\"Tidy-up-ifly!\"* he cried. Every sock flew into the air — and kept flying, out the window, all over town. By lunch, the mayor was wearing six of them. Theo spent all afternoon collecting socks by hand. In the end, his room was clean — and he learned some jobs are faster without magic."],
  ["tell me a bedtime story | bedtime story | tell me a story to help me sleep", "**The Sleepy Moon**\n\nOnce, the moon was too tired to rise. So the stars gathered and hummed a soft lullaby, the clouds tucked a fluffy blanket around her, and an owl whispered, *\"We'll keep watch tonight.\"* The moon smiled, glowed just a little, and drifted gently across the sky — slow and calm, like breathing. And everyone below, snug in their beds, drifted off too. Goodnight."],
  ["tell me a short story | write me a story | tell me a story | write a short story", "**The Last Leaf**\n\nOn a tall maple, one red leaf refused to fall. *\"Everyone else has gone,\"* said the wind. *\"Aren't you scared?\"* The leaf thought about it. *\"A little. But I've seen sunrise from up here every day.\"* When it finally let go, it didn't fall — it danced, twirling all the way down to land softly on a child's open palm. The child kept it in a book forever."],
  ["write a mystery story | mystery story for kids | tell me a mystery", "**The Case of the Missing Cookies**\n\nThe cookie jar was empty, and there were crumbs on the floor. Detective Maya studied the clues: the crumbs led to the couch, there was flour on the cushion, and a soft snore came from underneath. She lifted the cushion and found... Grandpa's dog, Waffles, belly full and covered in sugar. Case closed. Waffles got a bath; Maya got new cookies."],
  ["write a funny story | tell me a funny story | silly story", "**The Day the Chicken Went to School**\n\nOne morning, a chicken named Gertrude hopped on the school bus. The driver shrugged, so she rode along. In math, she pecked out the right answers. In music, she clucked perfectly in tune. At lunch, she traded corn for a cookie. When the principal asked who she was, Gertrude laid an egg on his desk and strutted out. Everyone agreed it was the best field trip ever — even though nobody went anywhere."],
  ["write a story about a superhero | superhero story | tell me a story about a hero", "**Captain Kindness**\n\nZoe didn't have super strength or laser eyes. Her only power was noticing when someone felt left out. At lunch, she sat with the new kid. At recess, she invited the quiet girl to play. Soon, the whole school started doing the same. Nobody gave Zoe a cape, but the new kid drew her one — and she wore it proudly."],
  ["write a story about the ocean | ocean story | story about a mermaid", "**Coral's Treasure**\n\nCoral the young mermaid collected shiny things from shipwrecks. One day she found a glass bottle with a note inside: *\"Hello, whoever finds this! I'm Sam, I'm eight, and I love the sea.\"* Coral couldn't write, so she filled the bottle with her prettiest shell and sent it back on the tide. Years later, Sam — now a marine scientist — still keeps that shell on her desk."],
  ["write a sonnet | give me a sonnet | can you write a sonnet", "**Sonnet for the Morning**\n\nThe morning slips in softly through the blind,\nand paints the wall in gold and gentle rose;\nit wakes the sleepy corners of my mind\nand tells my stubborn eyes it's time — they close.\nThe kettle starts to whistle down the hall,\nthe sparrows chatter gossip on the wire,\nthe cat stretches like nothing's wrong at all,\nthe sun climbs up the steeple, ever higher.\nI used to think the dawn was much too soon,\na thief that stole the warmth of quilt and bed;\nbut now I see it hums a hopeful tune\nand lays a brand-new day out just ahead.\n  So rise, my friend, and greet the early light —\n  each morning is a promise kept from night."],
  ["give me a writing prompt | writing prompts for kids | journal prompt ideas", "Here are a few prompts:\n• You wake up and can understand animals. What does your pet say first?\n• Describe your dream treehouse in detail.\n• Write a letter to yourself ten years from now.\n• A door appears in your backyard. Where does it lead?"],
  ["tell me a fun fact about words | fun fact about english | interesting word facts", "Fun fact: the word **set** has one of the most meanings in the English dictionary — hundreds of them! And *queue* is pronounced the same even if you remove its last four letters."],
  ["what is the shortest sentence in english | shortest complete sentence", "*Go.* is a complete sentence! Commands (imperatives) have an understood subject — *you* — so one word can be enough. *I am.* is another very short one."],
  ["what is a word that rhymes with orange | does anything rhyme with orange", "**Orange** has no perfect rhyme in common English. Close calls include *door hinge* and *sporange* (a rare botany word). Other famously rhyme-less words include *silver* and *month*."],
  ["give me words that rhyme with cat | words that rhyme with cat", "Words that rhyme with **cat**: *bat, hat, mat, sat, rat, flat, chat, that, splat, acrobat, habitat.*"],
  ["give me words that rhyme with day | words that rhyme with day", "Words that rhyme with **day**: *play, say, way, stay, gray, May, tray, sway, away, okay, display, holiday.*"],
  ["give me words that rhyme with love | words that rhyme with love", "Words that rhyme with **love**: *above, dove, glove, shove, of* — and near-rhymes like *enough* and *stuff*."],
  ["give me words that rhyme with night | words that rhyme with light", "Words that rhyme with **night**: *light, bright, kite, bite, fight, sight, white, height, tonight, delight, flight, polite.*"],
  ["how do you spell necessary | is it neccessary or necessary | spelling of necessary", "It's spelled **necessary** — one *c*, two *s*'s. Memory trick: *a shirt has one **c**ollar and two **s**leeves.*"],
  ["how do you spell separate | is it seperate or separate", "It's **separate** — there's *a rat* in sep-**a-rat**-e."],
  ["how do you spell definitely | is it definately or definitely", "It's **definitely** — think of the word *finite* hiding inside: de-**finite**-ly."],
  ["how do you spell receive | i before e except after c", "It's **receive** — following the rhyme *i before e, except after c*. The rule has exceptions (like *weird* and *science*), but it helps with words like *receive*, *ceiling* and *believe*."],
  ["what are the most commonly misspelled words | commonly misspelled words list", "Frequently misspelled: *necessary, separate, definitely, receive, accommodate, embarrass, occurrence, rhythm, calendar, tomorrow, beginning, believe, Wednesday* and *February*."]
);
